  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>5. Cost composition: origin vs destination and the double-count trap</h2>
    <p>
      A proper all-in schedule has an origin block, a destination block and a time-based block. The
      research snapshot verifies much of the destination block; the origin-side China block remains
      unpublished. The most expensive mistake is comparing a base freight rate with another quote's
      all-in figure, or accepting a lump sum that already includes a charge and then paying it again.
    </p>
    <DataTable
      columns={[
        { key: 'layer', label: 'Layer' },
        { key: 'basis', label: 'Verified / indicative basis' },
        { key: 'note', label: 'Note' },
        { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
      ]}
      rows={costRows}
      caption="Destination Europe carrier/terminal lines are partially verified with EUR values; origin China lines are not."
    />
    <SourceList sources={costSources} title="Sources — Europe hidden-charge cost composition" />

    <h3 class="mt-3">How to catch the base-rate-versus-all-in trap</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Ask whether the freight line is <strong>base</strong> or <strong>all-in</strong>.</li>
      <li>List THC, ISPS, documentation, VGM, congestion and special-equipment lines separately.</li>
      <li>Confirm the terminal free-time and demurrage free-day allowance in the same quote.</li>
      <li>Flag any line that appears twice: in the all-in figure and as an additional accessorial.</li>
      <li>For rail, apply the companion rule of thumb: +15–25% over base; for ocean Europe, prefer an explicit itemisation over a blanket percentage.</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="note mt-2">
      <strong>Why itemisation beats a lump sum:</strong> the same shipment can hide different
      assumptions about free time, congestion, special equipment and inland delivery. Keep the
      line-by-line schedule so post-arrival additions can be challenged against the original quote.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>6. Compliance points that change the fee stack</h2>
      <p>
        Compliance errors are the main reason fixed accessorials become daily holds. ISPS and VGM are
        mandatory under SOLAS; manifest corrections are cheaper before arrival; EORI/TARIC/VAT still
        apply at EU clearance; and INCOTERMS decide which party owns each charge.
      </p>
      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'rule', label: 'Rule' },
          { key: 'value', label: 'Verified / indicative value' },
          { key: 'note', label: 'Why it matters' },
          { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
        ]}
        rows={complianceRows}
        caption="ISPS and VGM fee values are MEDIUM confidence from the carrier tariff; EORI/TARIC/VAT and INCOTERMS are structural requirements."
      />
      <SourceList sources={complianceSources} title="Sources — Europe customs, SOLAS and VAT compliance" />

      <h3 class="mt-3">Pre-arrival checklist</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Submit the <strong>VGM</strong> on time; late VGM is EUR 80 in the verified tariff.</li>
        <li>Correct manifest data before arrival: pre-arrival EUR 40 versus post-arrival EUR 80.</li>
        <li>Confirm the <strong>EORI</strong> and correct <strong>TARIC/HS</strong> code before release.</li>
        <li>Match the quote lines to the chosen <strong>INCOTERM</strong>: EXW, FOB, CIF, DAP or DDP.</li>
        <li>Ask the broker to flag holds early rather than report them after free time has run.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

